<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Insurance claim terms</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms, Defined in Plain English</h1><p class="lede">43 words you’ll meet in Mississippi policies, estimates and appraisal awards — searchable, in plain English.</p></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><div class="gloss-tools"><label for="gloss-q">Search 43 terms</label><input id="gloss-q" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count aria-live="polite">43 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#t-A">A</a><a href="#t-C">C</a><a href="#t-D">D</a><a href="#t-F">F</a><a href="#t-H">H</a><a href="#t-I">I</a><a href="#t-L">L</a><a href="#t-M">M</a><a href="#t-N">N</a><a href="#t-O">O</a><a href="#t-P">P</a><a href="#t-R">R</a><a href="#t-S">S</a><a href="#t-T">T</a><a href="#t-U">U</a><a href="#t-W">W</a><a href="#t-X">X</a></nav><dl class="gloss"><div class="term" id="t-A"><dt>Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually figured as replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay this amount first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Additional living expense</dt><dd>Extra costs you incur to live elsewhere while your home is unlivable after a covered loss, such as rent, hotel bills or higher food costs, above your normal spending.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the covered damage. It is the question an appraisal panel answers; coverage and policy interpretation are separate questions.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Anti-concurrent causation clause</dt><dd>Policy language saying a loss is excluded if an excluded cause, such as flood, contributes to it, regardless of other causes acting at the same time. How it applies depends on the exact wording and facts.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision that sets the amount of loss, usually signed by both appraisers or by one appraiser and the umpire.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The section of a property policy that lets either side demand appraisal when you and the carrier disagree on the amount of a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>The written notice, sent by you or the carrier, that invokes the appraisal clause and starts the policy’s timelines for naming appraisers.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-C"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A clause, common on commercial policies, that can reduce a payment if the property was insured for less than a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Competent and disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>Typical policy wording for the qualifications each side’s appraiser must meet: able to value the loss and free of the kind of stake in the outcome that the policy forbids.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>The dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy, covering the house and attached structures. Percentage deductibles are usually figured from this limit.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, for detached buildings such as fences, sheds, detached garages and barns, often set as a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for contents such as furniture, clothing and electronics, which may be paid at actual cash value or replacement cost.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage D</dt><dd>Loss of use coverage, which includes additional living expense and, on some forms, fair rental value when a rented unit becomes unlivable.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy showing the named insured, the property, coverage limits, deductibles and the policy period.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss you pay before the policy pays. It is applied after the amount of loss is set.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition. It is subtracted from replacement cost to reach actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof edges to direct water into the gutters and away from the fascia. It is often required when a roof is replaced.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-F"><dt>Flood (NFIP)</dt><dd>Flood coverage, usually written through the National Flood Insurance Program, is separate from homeowners and wind policies and has its own claim rules.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation a carrier withholds from the first payment on a replacement cost claim until repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-I"><dt>Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof underlayment applied at eaves, valleys and penetrations to resist leaks from ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as removing and replacing one square of shingles, with a quantity, unit price and total.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs reasonably match in color, texture or style. The answer depends on policy wording.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mississippi Insurance Department</dt><dd>The state agency that regulates insurers doing business in Mississippi and accepts consumer complaints. It is headed by an elected Commissioner of Insurance.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps taken after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or drying out a wet room. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>MWUA</dt><dd>The Mississippi Windstorm Underwriting Association, a residual-market insurer of wind and hail coverage for property in the six southernmost counties.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-N"><dt>Named-storm deductible</dt><dd>A deductible that applies when a loss is caused by a named tropical storm or hurricane, as defined in the policy. It is often a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for the added cost of bringing repaired or rebuilt property up to current building codes. Limits are often a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Overhead and profit</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for managing a job and earning a margin, often figured as a percentage on top of repair costs when several trades are needed.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-P"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible stated as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount. On a larger home it can be a significant sum.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed and the facts of the loss. Some policies and flood programs require one within a set time.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A professional who represents a policyholder in presenting and negotiating a claim, typically for a percentage fee. An appraiser’s role is narrower and limited to the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation that a replacement cost policy will pay back once repairs are finished and documented, usually within a stated period.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without a deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which a carrier says it is handling a claim while keeping the right to deny coverage later based on policy terms.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Roof square</dt><dd>A unit of roof area equal to 100 square feet. Roofing materials and labor are usually priced per square.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what was damaged and what work is needed to repair it. Many appraisal disputes are really scope disputes.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on roofs with a steep pitch, often starting around a 7/12 slope, because the work is slower and needs more safety gear.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Storm surge</dt><dd>The rise in sea level pushed ashore by a hurricane’s winds. Surge damage is generally treated as flood, not wind, under most policies.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an existing estimate after more damage is found or prices change during repairs.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-T"><dt>Tree removal limit</dt><dd>A cap on payment for removing fallen trees, which on many homeowners forms is a set amount per tree and per occurrence, and may apply only when a tree strikes a covered structure or blocks access.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-W"><dt>Wind-driven rain</dt><dd>Rain forced into a building by wind. Many policies cover interior water damage only if wind first creates an opening in the roof or walls.</dd></div><div class="term" id="t-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by carriers, adjusters and contractors to price repairs line by line using regional price lists.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm CT</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">Appraisal roadmap</a></li><li><a href="/questions-answered/">Questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/" aria-current="page">Insurance claim terms</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
